1.1 Introduction to dataframes
==============================

The ‘dataframes’ package provides pandas-like tabular data for Maxima.
It extends the ‘numerics’ ndarray ecosystem with string columns and
mixed-type tables, enabling work with real-world datasets that combine
numeric and categorical data.

*Requirements:* SBCL (Steel Bank Common Lisp).  The ‘numerics’ package
is loaded automatically.

* Menu:

* Key concepts::
* Quick start::
* String columns::
* Mixed tables::
* Converting between tables and ndarrays::


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Key concepts,  Next: Quick start,  Up: Introduction to dataframes

1.1.1 Key concepts
------------------

*string-column* is a 1D column of strings, the text counterpart to
ndarray.  It uses 1-based indexing (matching Maxima convention) and
appears as an opaque handle, like ndarray.

*df_table* is a columnar table with named, typed columns.  Each column
is either an ndarray (for numeric data) or a string-column (for text
data).  Tables are the primary container for mixed-type tabular data.


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Quick start,  Next: String columns,  Prev: Key concepts,  Up: Introduction to dataframes

1.1.2 Quick start
-----------------

(%i1) load("dataframes")$
(%i2) names : df_string_column(["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"]);
(%o2)      string_column(3) ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"]
(%i3) scores : ndarray([95.0, 87.0, 92.0])$
(%i4) T : df_table(["name", "score"], [names, scores]);
(%o4)              df_table: 3 rows x 2 cols
(%i5) df_table_column(T, "name");
(%o5)      string_column(3) ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"]
(%i6) df_table_column(T, "score");
(%o6)            ndarray([3], DOUBLE-FLOAT)


