File: dataframes.info,  Node: Function df_mutate T col_name pred,  Next: Function df_summarize T name1 fn1 name2 fn2 ...,  Prev: Function df_rename T old_name new_name,  Up: String Columns

 -- Function: df_mutate (T, COL_NAME, PRED)
     Add or replace a column by applying a lambda row-wise.  Lambda
     parameter names are matched to column names.  If the column name
     already exists, it is replaced.

* Menu:

     * Examples::

1.3.1.25 Examples
.................

(%i1) T2 : df_mutate(T, "double", lambda([price], 2 * price))$
(%i2) df_table_names(T2);
(%o2)              [name, price, double]
(%i3) np_to_list(df_table_column(T2, "double"));
(%o3)                  [20.0, 60.0, 40.0]

See also: ‘df_filter’, ‘df_summarize’


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Function df_summarize T name1 fn1 name2 fn2 ...,  Next: Function df_distinct T / df_distinct T cols,  Prev: Function df_mutate T col_name pred,  Up: String Columns

 -- Function: df_summarize (T, NAME1, FN1, NAME2, FN2, ...)
     Reduce a table to a single-row summary.  Takes alternating
     name/lambda pairs.  Each lambda receives whole columns (as ndarray
     or string-column handles) and must return a scalar.

     Also works with grouped tables from ‘df_group_by’, producing one
     row per group.

* Menu:

     * Examples::

1.3.1.26 Examples
.................

(%i1) df_summarize(T, "avg", lambda([price], np_mean(price)),
                      "n",   lambda([price], df_count(price)));
(%o1)              df_table: 1 rows x 2 cols

See also: ‘df_mutate’, ‘df_group_by’, ‘df_describe’


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Function df_distinct T / df_distinct T cols,  Next: Function df_group_by T col_name,  Prev: Function df_summarize T name1 fn1 name2 fn2 ...,  Up: String Columns

 -- Function: df_distinct (T) / DF_DISTINCT (T, COLS)
     Return unique rows.  Optionally specify which columns to consider
     for uniqueness.

* Menu:

     * Examples::

1.3.1.27 Examples
.................

(%i1) T : df_table(["x"], [df_string_column(["a","b","a","c"])])$
(%i2) df_table_shape(df_distinct(T));
(%o2)                        [3, 1]

See also: ‘df_filter’, ‘df_nunique’ ## Grouped Tables

Group a table by a column for per-group aggregation with ‘df_summarize’.


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Function df_group_by T col_name,  Next: Function df_inner_join T1 T2 key,  Prev: Function df_distinct T / df_distinct T cols,  Up: String Columns

 -- Function: df_group_by (T, COL_NAME)
     Group a table by a column, returning a grouped table.  The grouped
     table can then be passed to ‘df_summarize’ to compute per-group
     statistics.

* Menu:

     * Examples::

1.3.1.28 Examples
.................

(%i1) T : df_table(["region", "revenue"],
                    [df_string_column(["N","S","N","S"]),
                     ndarray([10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0])])$
(%i2) G : df_group_by(T, "region")$
(%i3) df_summarize(G, "total", lambda([revenue], np_sum(revenue)));
(%o3)              df_table: 2 rows x 2 cols

The result table has the group-key column plus one column per summary
function.

See also: ‘df_summarize’, ‘df_describe’ ## Joins and Binds

Combine tables by key columns (joins) or by stacking (binds).


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Function df_inner_join T1 T2 key,  Next: Function df_left_join T1 T2 key,  Prev: Function df_group_by T col_name,  Up: String Columns

 -- Function: df_inner_join (T1, T2, KEY)
     Join two tables on a key column, keeping only rows with matching
     keys in both tables.

* Menu:

     * Examples::

1.3.1.29 Examples
.................

(%i1) T1 : df_table(["id", "name"],
                     [df_string_column(["1","2","3"]),
                      df_string_column(["Alice","Bob","Charlie"])])$
(%i2) T2 : df_table(["id", "score"],
                     [df_string_column(["1","2"]),
                      ndarray([90.0, 80.0])])$
(%i3) df_inner_join(T1, T2, "id");
(%o3)              df_table: 2 rows x 3 cols

See also: ‘df_left_join’, ‘df_bind_cols’


File: dataframes.info,  Node: Function df_left_join T1 T2 key,  Next: Function df_bind_rows T1 T2,  Prev: Function df_inner_join T1 T2 key,  Up: String Columns

 -- Function: df_left_join (T1, T2, KEY)
     Join two tables on a key column, keeping all rows from the left
     table.  Unmatched right columns are filled with 0 (numeric) or “”
     (string).

* Menu:

     * Examples::

1.3.1.30 Examples
.................

(%i1) df_left_join(T1, T2, "id");
(%o1)              df_table: 3 rows x 3 cols

See also: ‘df_inner_join’


